  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}+10,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=2$ is a critical point of $f(x)$\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(2)>0$\n- $x=2$ is a relative  minimum of $f(x)$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to identify whether $x=2$ is a critical point. If it is, we then need to classify it using the second derivative test\n\nFirst, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x)=2{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}+10,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=10{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{3}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have critical points at $x=0,2.$ We're interested in the critical point $x=2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at this critical point. Computing ${f}^{\u2033}(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=40{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand evaluating ${f}^{\u2033}(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(2)=40(2)^{3}-60(2)^{2}=80>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is positive at $x=2,$ so this critical point is a relative *minimum*.\n\nTherefore, comparing our results with the given statements, we conclude that:\n\n- $x=2$ is a critical point of $f(x)\\,\u2713$\n\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(2)>0\\,\u2713$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative *minimum* at $x=2\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
